  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 151 AM MDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nModels indicating another round of isolated storms this \nafternoon across the southern highlands. Cloud cover north of \nthe southern highlands does not look as impressive as it had the\nprevious few forecast runs. As a result, the high temperatures \nfor today are warm once again with highs in the low to id 90s \nfor many of our lower elevation communities. \n\nModels show showers persisting through the night across the southern \nhighlands as the deeper moisture pools to our south. On Wednesday \nclouds and showers begin to advance northward. The NBM pops are \nprobably underdone across the Magic Valley and Snake River Plain at \nthis point for Wednesday. Think there will be some isolated showers \nand thunderstorms but not overly confident yet. The big push of \nmoisture occurs Wednesday Night into Thursday. A long wave trough \nmoves onto the western U.S. coast while an embedded circulation \nejects out of California and into the northern Great Basin on \nThursday. Chances for showers and storms continues to look good. \nQuite a bit of cloud cover will likely be around which could limit \nthunderstorm development on Thursday. With that said, we continue a \nmarginal risk for Thursday through Saturday for heavy rain. \nEssentially if a strong storm finds a burn scar or poor drainage \narea then could see some isolated flooding. Right now, the \nthunderstorm potential looks better Friday/Saturday with the models \nshowing larger breaks in the clouds to allow for morning and early \nafternoon heating. Although in these types of strong monsoon pushes, \ntiming the cloud cover from previous day's convection is tricky, \nespecially several days out.",
